WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.060 --> 00:00:04.440
STEWART BRAND: So here's another in a sense national policy question from Kevin Kelly: can we

00:00:04.440 --> 00:00:10.889
learn anything about reforming capitalism from China?

00:00:10.889 --> 00:00:13.469
MARIANA MAZZUCATO: My previous book was called The Entrepreneurial State. And when I think

00:00:13.469 --> 00:00:20.430
of China, I think of the 'Adaptive State.'
China, really interestingly I think, is

00:00:20.430 --> 00:00:27.210
learning from the US in a moment that
the US. is unlearning from itself.

00:00:27.210 --> 00:00:31.949
It struck me that the first thing Trump did when he came into office was

00:00:31.949 --> 00:00:38.040
actually attacked particular public
organizations: ARPA-E and PBS. And what

00:00:38.040 --> 00:00:41.579
China's been doing—and I think
there's a question or whether it's gonna

00:00:41.579 --> 00:00:47.489
be successful or not—is forming
actually quite active investor

00:00:47.489 --> 00:00:52.500
innovative public organizations. But
they're huge. They're huge. It's not a

00:00:52.500 --> 00:00:55.440
decentralized network of public
organizations across the whole

00:00:55.440 --> 00:01:00.090
innovation chain, as we had in the US. So
the Chinese Development Bank is a

00:01:00.090 --> 00:01:05.580
massive financial institution which has
provided loans up to nine billion for

00:01:05.580 --> 00:01:08.850
many of its companies, including Huawei. Huawei wouldn't exist without the

00:01:08.850 --> 00:01:12.270
Chinese Development Bank. By the way,
Huawei, which is on the news a lot, is a

00:01:12.270 --> 00:01:17.400
cooperative. Can you believe that?

00:01:17.400 --> 00:01:23.670
In Dalian, I met—they had a tea
ceremony in Dalian, China because the

00:01:23.670 --> 00:01:27.840
World Economic Forum has a youth
component that meets and Dalian—and

00:01:27.840 --> 00:01:31.920
Huawei executives asked me and three
other economists—this was back in, I

00:01:31.920 --> 00:01:37.079
think, 2013—to meet with them to say what should we do about the US? It was very

00:01:37.079 --> 00:01:40.140
strange, thinking about what's
happening now. And we were like, 'What do you mean?' They were like,

00:01:40.140 --> 00:01:44.220
'Well yeah, it's really hard in the US,
we're not really penetrating the

00:01:44.220 --> 00:01:49.170
market.' And my advice was: 'Tell them you're a cooperative.' I mean just in terms of

00:01:49.170 --> 00:01:54.899
getting...what I think is interesting in China is there's a lot of

00:01:54.899 --> 00:01:58.860
variety; there's not one model. There's
lots of different types of organizations,

00:01:58.860 --> 00:02:01.200
hence the Huawei example. It's a
cooperative. There's state-owned

00:02:01.200 --> 00:02:04.799
companies, there's companies that are
actually going more towards—slowly I

00:02:04.799 --> 00:02:10.379
think—a more market-driven and
anglo-saxon model, almost. But there's

00:02:10.379 --> 00:02:13.400
variety there. There's also much more
meritocracy

00:02:13.400 --> 00:02:16.700
in terms of who works in governments than we have in many

00:02:16.700 --> 00:02:19.970
countries. In the UK there's very little
meritocracy in terms of who

00:02:19.970 --> 00:02:24.439
actually rises up in government. There's
lots of experiments actually at the

00:02:24.439 --> 00:02:28.790
community level but it also has problems of

00:02:28.790 --> 00:02:34.549
democracy. So it's not a cut and paste model from

00:02:34.549 --> 00:02:40.250
communist versus capitalist. But
how it evolves, the degree to which there

00:02:40.250 --> 00:02:43.849
is engagement...for example,
they're spending 1.7 trillion on

00:02:43.849 --> 00:02:48.769
greening their economy, coming back to
the Green missions. And this is out of

00:02:48.769 --> 00:02:52.940
urgency. And it's really
interesting how also in the US,. big

00:02:52.940 --> 00:02:56.959
things happen when there is urgency, when there's war. And if you've seen

00:02:56.959 --> 00:03:00.590
also the documentary on the Roosevelts,
it's fantastic. When there was urgency,

00:03:00.590 --> 00:03:04.909
big things happen in the US. And so the
degree to which this urgency creates

00:03:04.909 --> 00:03:09.799
—because of pollution—is creating both
a very positive change—so 1.7 trillion

00:03:09.799 --> 00:03:13.519
on greening the economy including energy-friendly technologies, not just renewable

00:03:13.519 --> 00:03:19.400
energy—but also that rush to
create and invest and not focus on

00:03:19.400 --> 00:03:23.480
really rebalancing the whole economy, for
example, around consumption and services

00:03:23.480 --> 00:03:28.419
is is causing the economy to be
unbalanced.

